{
  "concept": "Jarvis's march (gift wrapping) builds the convex hull one vertex at a time by always choosing, from the current hull vertex, the point subtending the smallest polar angle from a horizontal reference ray — sweeping up the right chain with rays pointing right and back down the left chain with rays pointing left.",
  "figureType": "diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Scattered planar point set: 13 filled black dots total",
    "Five convex-hull vertices drawn as larger filled black dots, labeled p_0 (bottom left), p_1 (lower right), p_2 (right), p_3 (top), p_4 (left)",
    "Eight unlabeled interior points scattered inside the polygon",
    "Convex hull polygon drawn as a closed thick gray band with a thin dark outline, through p_0 → p_1 → p_2 → p_3 → p_4 → p_0",
    "Dashed straight line passing through p_0 and p_3, dividing the point set into left and right chains",
    "Short arrow pair with text 'left chain' (arrow pointing left) and 'right chain' (arrow pointing right) straddling the dashed line above p_3",
    "Identical 'left chain' / 'right chain' arrow-and-label pair straddling the dashed line below p_0",
    "Short vertical tick mark at each of the five hull vertices",
    "Horizontal reference ray at p_0, p_1, p_2 pointing right (with arrowhead)",
    "Horizontal reference ray at p_4 and p_3 pointing left (with arrowhead)",
    "Thin circular arc at each hull vertex sweeping from the reference ray to the outgoing hull edge: very narrow arc at p_0, moderate at p_1, nearly a half turn at p_2, wide arc at p_4, moderate arc at p_3",
    "Angle value readouts (degrees) appearing next to the active arc during stepping",
    "Candidate ray and candidate-angle readout shown on hover over an interior point",
    "Legend/caption strip naming the active step and chain"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "marchStep",
      "type": "button",
      "label": "Step the march",
      "teaches": "That each hull vertex is found by picking the point with the minimum polar angle measured from the current vertex's reference ray, and that repeating this wraps the entire point set.",
      "states": ["start at p0", "at p0 → find p1", "at p1 → find p2", "at p2 → find p3", "at p3 → find p4", "at p4 → close to p0", "hull complete"],
      "default": "start at p0"
    },
    {
      "id": "chainView",
      "type": "toggle",
      "label": "Chain emphasis",
      "teaches": "Why the reference direction flips at the lowest and highest points: the right chain is wrapped with rightward rays going up, the left chain with leftward rays coming back down.",
      "options": ["right chain", "left chain", "both chains"],
      "default": "both chains"
    },
    {
      "id": "candidateHover",
      "type": "hover",
      "teaches": "That every remaining point — interior or not — is a legal candidate, but each interior point subtends a larger polar angle than the winner, so it is never selected.",
      "label": "Hover a point to compare its candidate angle"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "The point set and its hull",
      "narration": "Thirteen points sit in the plane; the thick gray polygon through p_0, p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4 is the convex hull we want to discover. A dashed line through the lowest point p_0 and the highest point p_3 splits the boundary into a right chain and a left chain.",
      "state": { "marchStep": "start at p0", "chainView": "both chains" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Start at the lowest point",
      "narration": "The march begins at p_0, guaranteed to be on the hull because it is lowest. A horizontal ray points right from p_0, and every other point's polar angle above that ray is measured.",
      "state": { "marchStep": "start at p0", "chainView": "right chain" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Smallest angle wins: p_1",
      "narration": "The arc at p_0 is very narrow — p_1 subtends the smallest polar angle of all candidates, so the edge p_0p_1 joins the hull. Hover an interior point to see its larger angle and why it loses.",
      "state": { "marchStep": "at p0 → find p1", "chainView": "right chain" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Wrapping up the right chain",
      "narration": "From p_1 the rightward ray sweeps again and picks p_2; from p_2 the arc is nearly a half turn before it reaches p_3. Each new vertex is found with the same minimum-angle rule.",
      "state": { "marchStep": "at p2 → find p3", "chainView": "right chain" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Flip at the top: the left chain",
      "narration": "p_3 is the highest point, so the right chain ends. Now the reference ray points left, and the same minimum-angle rule walks back down through p_4.",
      "state": { "marchStep": "at p3 → find p4", "chainView": "left chain" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Close the loop",
      "narration": "From p_4 the leftward ray sweeps a wide arc to p_0, closing the polygon. All eight interior points were examined at every step and rejected every time.",
      "state": { "marchStep": "hull complete", "chainView": "both chains" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Gift wrapping never needs to sort: at each hull vertex the next one is simply the point of minimum polar angle from a fixed reference ray, and flipping that ray from rightward to leftward at the extreme points lets the same rule wrap both chains of the hull.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Wide landscape layout on white. A convex pentagon fills most of the frame: p_0 low-left-of-center, p_1 lower right, p_2 far right slightly above middle, p_3 at the top just left of center, p_4 at the far left middle. Hull drawn as a thick light-gray band with a thin dark outline; hull vertices are bold black dots with serif italic labels p_0…p_4 placed just outside the polygon. Eight smaller black dots scatter irregularly through the interior. A thin dashed line runs from below p_0 up through p_3 and slightly beyond, tilted steeply. Above p_3 and below p_0, a pair of short horizontal arrows with the serif labels 'left chain' (pointing left) and 'right chain' (pointing right) straddle the dashed line. Each hull vertex carries a short vertical tick crossing it and a long thin horizontal ray with an arrowhead — rightward at p_0, p_1, p_2, leftward at p_4 and p_3 — plus a thin circular arc from the ray around to the outgoing hull edge (tiny arc at p_0, medium at p_1, near-180° at p_2, wide at p_4, medium at p_3). For interactive states, highlight the active vertex, its ray, its arc, and the newly added edge in a saturated accent color (e.g. orange), dim inactive hull edges to pale gray, and draw hovered-candidate rays as thin dashed accent lines with a small degree readout label."
}

